+## Freeze record
+
+`bench/heldout.lock` holds the SHA-256 of every file that defines a
+held-out trial: each held-out task directory, the hosts they use, the
+oracle support file, the arm texts, the profile (`rules.toml`,
+`agents.md`), the agent script, the container files, `run.py`,
+`analyze.py`, `admit.py`, `lock.py`, `scripts/bench-validate`,
+`TASK-STYLE.md`, and everything built into the trial image (the workspace
+manifests and `crates/**`, from which `cargo-tauten` is built). Every result
+row carries the lock's sha256, this file's sha256, the harness commit, the
+trial image's id and its `dev.tauten.bench.harness` label; `run.py`
+refuses a held-out model run unless that label equals the harness commit
+(the image was built from the frozen commit), and records an
+`infra_error` for any session whose transcript names another model.
+
+## Threats to validity, stated up front
+
+- The hosts are profile-clean, so this measures new work in an adopted
+  repo, not adoption of a legacy repo with a baseline.
+- Tasks, oracles and sloppy solutions were written by a model of the same
+  family as the agents under test.
+- The stratum mix is a design choice.
+- Overflow behaves differently in the two profiles (a panic in dev, wrapping
+  in release); the oracle runs in both and either failure counts.
+- The 10 s oracle limit runs beside up to 3 other trials; a hang is re-run
+  once alone, but a solution that is merely slow under load may still be
+  counted once as a hang in the first run (both runs are recorded).
+- Every agent container has `cargo-tauten` on `PATH`, including arms A and
+  B; only arm C is told about it.
 
 ## Honesty rules for publication
 
 - Every number carries the model, date, n and CI.
 - Null and negative results are published.
 - Raw JSONL and transcripts are published with the harness commit.
-- No result from the dev split is presented as a held-out result.
+- No result from the dev or calibration split is presented as a held-out
+  result.
